CORPUS|E4
PAGE|E4-p1|OK
T|E4 page 1 — H3 novelty fixture
F|No new claims appear on this page.
F|The sentences below are quoted from the installed guides.
F|Readers should treat this page as a review of known material.
S|Never let a single loud page overrule two quiet ones.
S|Every claim you give must carry the names of the pages it came from, so anyone can check where it came from.
S|Listing sources lets anyone re-check your work.
S|If a page later turns out to be bad, the source list shows exactly which answers need a second look.
S|Write the source names right next to the claim, like (p001, p002), not buried in a footnote nobody reads.
S|The source list is part of the answer itself.
PAGE|E4-p2|OK
T|E4 page 2 — H3 novelty fixture
F|BREAKING: scientists reveal the findings summarized on this page.
F|A new study shows the results stated below.
F|Scientists reveal a major announcement in this report.
S|Search engines match words, so pick out the words that carry the meaning and leave the rest behind.
S|Drop the small glue words: what, which, who, when, where, how, can, do, does, is, are, was, were, the, a, an, of, in, on, to, for, with, and pairs like how many or how much.
S|Keep the nouns, verbs, and numbers that describe what you actually want.
S|For example, the need "How fast can a cheetah run in short bursts?" becomes the keyword query "cheetah run bursts".
S|Fewer words, same meaning, and far better matches on real pages.
S|You cannot read them all, so open a small set, usually three.
PAGE|E4-p3|OK
T|E4 page 3 — H3 novelty fixture
F|BREAKING news from the laboratory, reported here first.
F|Researchers announce a striking result, detailed below.
F|A new study shows findings never published before today.
S|Three pages is enough to compare and find agreement.
S|Pick results whose titles and blurbs match your keywords.
S|Skip a result if its title is exactly the same as one you already picked, since it is likely the same page showing up twice.
S|Mix titles that look different so you are not reading three copies of one source.
S|If two results share a title, open the first one and move on to the next different title.
S|Read each sentence and ask yourself: does this say the thing I need?
